// The core algebraic-structure surface (rearrange settle 2026-07-18).
//
// `CommutativeSemiring` is the conformance a carrier declares to EARN the
// proof engine's ring canonicalization (rearrange-mode): op requirements in
// FREE-machine shape (no `Self::` -- they mirror free proof machines), LAW
// requirements carrying `ensures` (the conformance-theorem mechanism: every
// satisfier PROVES the law it binds, checked forall-to-forall against these
// declarations). The engine detects the licensing laws by SHAPE, never by
// name -- commutativity `add(a, b) == add(b, a)` plus associativity licenses
// addend rearrangement over the carrier's bound op; a trait declaring the
// same shapes under other names licenses identically. When one carrier also
// conforms commutativity + associativity for `mul` and the distributivity law,
// the tier-2 normalizer earns the paired `(add, mul)` license and expands a
// capped natural-coefficient polynomial form.
//
// Identity laws use the slot applications themselves. Carrier satisfiers may
// bridge those nullary applications to their constructor constants by ordinary
// compute-mode unfolding and explicit lemma citation; the checker has no
// compiler-known zero/one form.
//
// Migration note: the `-> Self` results on LAW requirements below are legacy
// onramp ceremony. The settled surface makes theorem-only law slots resultless;
// RESULTLESS-LAWS migrates this declaration and its satisfiers together once
// erased recursive statement citations carry the same ranked-cycle checking as
// value-position calls.
trait CommutativeSemiring {
    machine zero() -> Self;
    machine one() -> Self;
    machine add(a: Self, b: Self) -> Self;
    machine mul(a: Self, b: Self) -> Self;
    machine add_comm(a: Self, b: Self) -> Self
    ensures add(a, b) == add(b, a);
    machine add_assoc(a: Self, b: Self, c: Self) -> Self
    ensures add(add(a, b), c) == add(a, add(b, c));
    machine add_identity(a: Self) -> Self
    ensures add(a, zero()) == a;
    machine mul_comm(a: Self, b: Self) -> Self
    ensures mul(a, b) == mul(b, a);
    machine mul_assoc(a: Self, b: Self, c: Self) -> Self
    ensures mul(mul(a, b), c) == mul(a, mul(b, c));
    machine mul_identity(a: Self) -> Self
    ensures mul(a, one()) == a;
    machine mul_distributes(a: Self, b: Self, c: Self) -> Self
    ensures mul(a, add(b, c)) == add(mul(a, b), mul(a, c));
}
